[FEN "4k3/p1p2ppp/4p3/1p1pP3/3P1P2/2P5/PP4PP/4K3 b - - 0 1"]

 { A pawn break is 'a pawn advance to a square attacked by an enemy pawn, with the intent of opening the position or challenging the opponent's pawn structure.' This can lead to many positional benefits. Typically these include opening lines, improving your structure or damaging the opponent's, as well as contesting the centre and generating attacking chances on either flank. Let's now consider the benefits of a pawn break from Black's perspective, and then in the next example we'll keep the same position but look at it from the White side instead. One last thing, this is a barebones structure that has been stripped of all the pieces. In reality, this will have a huge impact on whether the pawn break should be played or not. } 1... { -KEY- } c5 { Black breaks with 1...c5, and White is left with two options: a) Capture it b) Ignore it } 2. Ke2 { White chooses to ignore the break. In this case Black has three options: ...b4, ...c4, and ...cxd4. } ( 2. dxc5 { If White chooses to capture: - White's central control has been undermined as they no longer have a pawn on d4. - If Black is in a position to recapture on c5 with a piece, this may lead to greater piece activity. - If White's king is sitting on the g1 square (having castled short), then White's king safety will also be significantly undermined. - Black now has two follow-up pawn breaks: ...b5-b4, and ...d5-d4. } ) 
cxd4 ( 2... b4 { A second pawn break allows Black to threaten to open up not just the c-file, but also the b-file. } ) 
 ( 2... c4 { It now becomes a lot harder for White to ever move the b2 pawn, since a move like b2-b3 may result in ...cxb3, leaving a very vulnerable undefended pawn on c3 along a semi-open file. } ) 
3. cxd4 { Black now has an open c-file. Note how the side that plays the pawn break has the power to choose if and when to open up the file, depending on what favors their pieces the most. Let's flip perspective, this time with White to move. } 
 *

