[FEN "4k3/p1p2ppp/4p3/1p1pP3/3P1P2/2P5/PP4PP/4K3 w - - 0 1"]

 { This is the same structure, but now we'll take a look at the main pawn break that White can play. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { White carries out their own pawn break. It's worth noting that this is not the only pawn break in the position. Depending on White's piece arrangement, 1.a4, or even preparing a break on the c4 square may have been stronger. } ( { Assisted pawn break: } 1. g4 { White could also have 'assisted' the pawn break by pushing the g-pawn first.This can add potency and even more favourable outcomes for the attacking side. For instance: } Kd8 2. f5 exf5 3. gxf5 { The change in structure favors White, as they now have the semi-open g-file to maneuver along. } ) 
Kf8 { What happens if Black ignores the pawn break? } ( 1... exf5 { The capture weakens the d5 pawn, particularly significant if Black did not have a pawn on the c-file to support it. If White could recapture on f5 with a piece, then White will now have several open lines of attack against Black's kingside to operate on. Other pieces could also have benefited e.g. a bishop on c1 or d2 would now enjoy greater range of movement. } 2. e6 { A second pawn break! In theory, White could now advance their e-pawn. Here it doesn't look like a very good idea since there are no pieces on the board. But in a real game, such sacrifices can offer a route to the enemy king. } ) 
2. f6 { White can also choose to advance the pawn further. In this case, the pawn on the 6th rank constitutes a so-called 'pawn wedge' (covered elsewhere in the course). Such pawns are highly dangerous as they can quickly assist in forming mating nets around the enemy king. } ( 2. fxe6 { White can exchange at a moment that suits them, cracking open the f-file. } fxe6 { White will often have a rook on f1, so this opening up is very dangerous. Note also how the e6 pawn no longer has a base pawn to defend it. Black must now guard their e-pawn with pieces for the remainder of the game, a constant liability to worry about! } ) 
gxf6 ( 2... g6 { Black doesn't want to crack their structure, but in avoiding this they have left themselves with pawns all on light squares. Sometimes pawn breaks are designed as part of an attack along specific 'color complexes', a topic discussed in the chapter on 'Weak Squares'. } ) 
3. exf6 { Black has been left with a fractured pawn structure. Additionally, the e5 square is now a potential outpost for a White piece, as well as the h2-b8 diagonal having opened up. Hopefully the discussion of this 'barebones' structure has been helpful to build a solid picture of the types of benefits that come with pawn breaks, and to understand why experienced players often build positional plans around pawn breaks. Let's turn to some real world examples next. } 
 *

