 { One of the advantages of a well-timed pawn break is that it transforms the structure in a way that is favorable for the attacking side. In this example, taken from the game Schlechter,C - Janowski,D (1900), White uses a pawn break as a sacrificial tool in order to cripple Black's pawn structure. } 31. { -KEY- } e6 $1 dxe6 ( { Forced, since otherwise }  32. exd7 { would win material. } ) 
32. Qe5 $1 { The pawn break has left Black with isolated pawns on the c- and e-files, more than enough compensation for the sacrificed pawn. Additionally, the d-file has opened up for White's rook. Finally, the White knight on c5 now sits on a permanently weak square. The combination of factors means that White already enjoys a winning position. } Qe7 33. g4 Rb4 34. Kg3 { White patiently improves his pieces. There is no need to rush when your opponent has no active plan. } Rb6 35. h4 Qf8 36. h5 $1 { A second pawn break, this time designed to open lines of attack against the Black king. } gxh5 37. Qxh5 Rb8 38. Qe5 Rb6 39. g5 $1 { And now a third break! Soon Black's king will not be able to find shelter. } h5 40. g6+ $1 Kxg6 41. Qg5+ Kh7 42. Qxh5+ Kg8 43. Qg5 ( { The effect of White's kingside pawn breaks is clear. Black has no good way to meet the threat of } 43. Qg5  44. Rd8 { . } ) 
Kf7 44. Rd8 Qe7 45. Qh5+ ( { Black resigned in view of the line } 45. Qh5+ g6 46. Qh7 Kf6 47. Qh8 Kf7 48. Qg8 Kf6 49. Rf8 Qxf8 50. Qxf8 { . In this game we saw that White had a crushing position from the get-go. From here he found the best strategy to convert his advantage, using a series of pawn breaks to open lines, improve his piece activity, damage the opponent's pawn chain, and finally to hunt down the king. Pawn breaks are hard to match when it comes to the breadth of benefits they can offer! } ) 

