 { This position is taken from an online game between Magnus Carlsen, with the White pieces, and Alexei Sarana with the Black. Can you find the best sequence for Black? } 1... { -KEY- } f4 $1 ( 1... b6 $2 { In the game, Black was focused on stopping White from playing the c4-c5 pawn break, but in so doing they failed to spot their own active pawn break on the kingside! } ) 
2. h5 { This move is the only way that White can avoid losing a pawn. } ( 2. g4 { allows Black to play either } Bxh4 { winning a pawn, or continue his pawn break strategy with } e4 $5 { , clearing the e5 square and setting up a pawn advance either on e3 or f3 next. } 3. fxe4 f3 4. Bf1 Nxh4 $17 { Black has a monster pawn on f3 and dangerous moves like ...Ng2+ or ...Bg5, while their d7 knight will soon occupy the powerful e5 outpost. } ) 
fxg3 $1 { Correct! White's structure has been ruined, while now Black has a powerful outpost on f4 to take aim at. } 3. Bxg3 ( 3. hxg6 $2 gxf2 4. Kxf2 $19 { is hopeless for White, as the king has no shelter and is likely to get hunted down along the weakened dark squares. } ) 
Bg5 4. Qc2 Nf4 $19 { Black is winning, with devastatingly active minor pieces on the f4 and g5 squares, gained as a result of the pawn break. } 
