 { Another common benefit of pawn breaks has to do with undermining the centre. Oftentimes this can be simply reducing one side's central control. In more extreme cases like the following example, a well-timed break can cause a full collapse of the opponent's centre. } 1... { -KEY- } c5 $1 { The presence of White's knight on c3 stops them from being able to maintain their central pawn chain. } ( 1... O-O $2 { If Black had not seized the opportunity then White would have had the time to reorganize themselves in anticipation of the possible pawn break. } 2. Ne2 $1 c5 3. c3 $11 { With the centre stable, the position is roughly equal. } ) 
2. Be3 $2 { In the game White tried to defend their pawn, but now their bishop will come under fire. } ( 2. Ne2 { White is not in time now to regroup, since Black can play ...cxd4 after which the pawn on e5 is defenseless. } cxd4 $17 ) 
 ( 2. Qg4 $1 { was the only way to stay in the game, when after } g6 3. dxc5 Nxe5 4. Qe2 Nbc6 $15 { Black stands better, but White keeps practical chances due to having forced the weakening move 2...g6. } ) 
cxd4 3. Bxd4 Nc6 $17 { Black is close to winning since the e5 pawn is hard to keep alive. Additionally, Black can capture White's valuable dark-squared bishop, leaving White vulnerable on squares like e5, f2 and b2. } 
