 { The nature of pawn breaks is such that you can often gain several benefits at once. In this position, White finds a nasty pawn break to damage the opponent's structure and at the same time activate their pieces. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { White offers up a pawn on f5, confronting Black with the difficult decision of whether to capture or ignore the pawn. Note how White's dark-squared bishop on c1 is now much more active. } exf5 ( 1... Qb6 $6 { Ignoring the pawn break is worse: } 2. Nf4 $1 cxd4 3. cxd4 $16 { Black has no good way of defending the pawn on e6, while the move } exf5 { loses to } 4. Nxd5 { . } ) 
2. Nf4 a6 { Defending against Bxb5. } 3. Bd3 { White targets the vulnerable f-pawn. } ( 3. Nxd5 $2 { would lose a lot of the advantage, since now after a move like } Bb7 $13 { Black would have activated their light-squared bishop. } ) 
g6 4. a4 $1 { A second break, this time on the flank, puts the Black queenside pawns under pressure. } b4 ( 4... bxa4 $2 5. Qxa4 $16 { White's piece activity becomes too great here. } ) 
5. O-O { Black's position is problematic: 1. White has constant pressure on the d5 pawn 2. White is threatening a dangerous second pawn break, with the move e5-e6 3. Black cannot contest White's advanced e5 pawn, since the move ...f6 would leave the e6 square too weak. } cxd4 6. cxd4 Bb7 $2 { Trying to develop, but the position now collapses: } 7. e6 $1 { This idea of playing two pawn breaks (f5, and then e6) was harmless in the barebones structure at the beginning of the chapter, but with the Black king in the middle of the board it proves decisive. } Nf6 8. Ng5 fxe6 9. Nfxe6 Qe7 10. Re1 { Threatening Nc7+ or Nxf8. } Ne4 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Bxe4 $1 { A well-timed piece sacrifice, opening up the central files. } fxe4 13. Rxe4 $18 { Black's queen is lost. } Rc8 14. Bg5 $1 ( 14. Nc7 $2 { would throw away the advantage since after } Rxc7 15. Rxe7 Bxe7 $15 { Black would have too much material for the queen. } ) 
Qd6 15. Nxf8+ { The king is so vulnerable that already it is forced mate. For example: } Kxf8 16. Bh6+ Kf7 17. Qb3+ Kf6 18. Rf1+ Qf4 19. Rfxf4# { In this example we saw the powerful link between pawn breaks as a tool of positional play, and the transition to dynamic attacking chess. Let's take a look at one more example of this next. } 
