 { In this example, White could play a defensive move like Bc1-e3 but in the long run their isolated queen's pawn on d4 may become a liability. Instead, they use a pawn break to open lines since they have the better development. } 13. { -KEY- } d5 $1 { White is seeking to open up the a2-g8 diagonal for their bishop, as well as the e-file for their e1 rook. } exd5 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 $6 { Inviting the White queen into play will prove fatal. } ( 14... Ra7 { This difficult-to-find move was the only way to stay alive. For example: } 15. Be3 Rd7 16. Nxe7 Nxe7 17. Qc2 $14 { White has won the bishop pair, and stands slightly better. However, Black's compact pawn structure offers chances to hold the balance. } ) 
15. Qxd5 Bb7 ( 15... Qxd5 $2 { loses a piece after } 16. Bxd5 $18 ) 
16. Qh5 $1 { The pawn break at the start of the position forced Black's e6 pawn off the board, leaving the f7 pawn as an easy target. } Qc7 17. Bg5 Bxg5 18. Nxg5 h6 19. Re3 $1 Na5 $2 ( { Black cannot win a piece with } 19... hxg5 { , since after } 20. Rh3 $18 { there is no way to stop mate along the h-file. } ) 
 ( 19... Ne5 { was the only way to hang on, but probably Black didn't like that after } 20. Ne6 $1 fxe6 21. Rxe5 $16 { the pawn on e6 will soon fall. } ) 
20. Qg6 $3 { Beautiful tactical vision. Now Black is forced to capture on g5 because of the threat of mate. } hxg5 21. Rh3 Rfc8 22. Re1 { Cutting off the e-file and setting up the unstoppable threat of Qh7+ followed by Qh8#. Black resigned in Zvjaginsev - Schroeder, 2013. The combination of weakening of Black's pawn structure coupled with a quick activation of the White pieces made the pawn break in our starting position particularly venomous. Besides paving the way for an attack, a pawn break can also offer a path to breaking down an opponent's stubborn defense. Let's take a look at an example of this in the final position of the section. } 
