 { In this example (taken from an online game) , Magnus Carlsen with the White pieces has a clear positional advantage against GM Darius Swiercz. Can you find the crushing sequence that Magnus used in order to break down Black's defenses? } 1. { -KEY- } c5 $1 { At first glance, the pawn break seems impossible as the square is well-covered. However, this turns out to not be the case! } dxc5 ( 1... bxc5 { leads to a similar outcome after } 2. b6 $1 cxb6 3. Qxd6 $18 { Black cannot defend the queenside pawns, while White's light-squared bishop can soon enter the attack against the enemy king. } ) 
2. d6 $1 { Pawn breaks often chain together, and it's not rare that it is the second break that does more damage than the first. } cxd6 3. Qxd6 { Correct! There is no good way to defend the queenside pawns, and to make matters worse the Black king now lacks a safe place to hide. } Nc8 4. Qc6+ Kd8 ( 4... Qd7 { drops the bishop to } 5. Qxf6 $18 ) 
5. Bf5 $18 { Black can no longer hold the position together. In this section we have introduced pawn breaks and discussed the main benefits that they can bring to the attacking side. Next up, we'll flip perspective and take a look at how to defend against this powerful positional strategy. } 
